1
00:00:03,193 --> 00:00:05,310
Peter Alan Tyler, my dad.

2
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:07,546
The most wonderful man in the world.

3
00:00:07,586 --> 00:00:10,947
Born 15th September 1954.

4
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:16,587
Come here, Rose. Come here.

5
00:00:22,054 --> 00:00:25,330
Who's that? It's your daddy.

6
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:29,483
You weren't old enough
to remember when he died.

7
00:00:29,523 --> 00:00:31,200
1987.

8
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:33,320
7th of November.

9
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,357
D'you remember what I told you?

10
00:00:35,397 --> 00:00:39,835
The day that Stuart Hoskins
and Sarah Clark got married.

11
00:00:39,875 --> 00:00:42,672
He was always having adventures.

12
00:00:42,712 --> 00:00:45,869
Ah, he would have loved
to have seen you now.

13
00:00:48,386 --> 00:00:50,382
That's what Mum always says.

14
00:00:50,422 --> 00:00:53,990
So I was thinking. . .could we?

15
00:00:53,139 --> 00:00:56,535
Could we go and see my dad,
when he was still alive?

16
00:00:56,575 --> 00:00:58,252
Where's this come from
all of a sudden?

17
00:00:58,292 --> 00:01:01,607
If we can't, if it goes against the
laws of time or something,

18
00:01:01,647 --> 00:01:05,851
never mind, just leave it.
No, I can do anything.
I'm just more worried about you.

19
00:01:05,891 --> 00:01:07,331
I wanna see him.

20
00:01:07,371 --> 00:01:08,882
Your wish is my command.

21
00:01:08,922 --> 00:01:10,770
But be careful what you wish for. . .

22
00:01:52,356 --> 00:01:58,627
I, Peter Alan Tyler, take you, 
Jacqueline Andrea Suzette Prentice. . .

23
00:01:58,667 --> 00:02:00,908
I, Peter Alan Tyler,

24
00:02:00,948 --> 00:02:04,310
take you, Jacqueline Susanne. . .

25
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:07,586
Suzette. . . Anita?

26
00:02:11,656 --> 00:02:15,500
Oh, just carry on.
If it's good enough for Lady Di. . .

27
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:19,400
I thought he'd be taller.
To be my lawful wedded wife.

28
00:02:19,044 --> 00:02:23,161
To love and behold
till death us do part. . .

29
00:02:23,201 --> 00:02:24,957
'He died so close to home.'

30
00:02:24,997 --> 00:02:29,755
I wasn't there, nobody was.

31
00:02:29,795 --> 00:02:32,431
It was a hit-and-run driver.

32
00:02:32,471 --> 00:02:38,385
Never found out who. He was dead
when the ambulance got there.

33
00:02:38,425 --> 00:02:42,820
I only wish there'd been
someone there for him.

34
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:47,823
'I want to be that someone.

35
00:02:47,863 --> 00:02:50,140
'So he doesn't die alone.'

36
00:02:50,054 --> 00:02:52,801
November the 7th? 1987.

37
00:03:07,273 --> 00:03:11,369
MUSIC: "Never Can Say Goodbye"
by the Communards

38
00:03:17,034 --> 00:03:19,302
That's so weird.

39
00:03:19,342 --> 00:03:21,270
The day my father died.

40
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:26,335
 .I thought it would be
all sort of grim and stormy.
It's just an ordinary day.

41
00:03:26,375 --> 00:03:28,734
The past is another country.

42
00:03:28,774 --> 00:03:31,111
1987 is just the Isle of Wight.

43
00:03:32,853 --> 00:03:34,901
You sure about this? Yeah.

44
00:03:47,198 --> 00:03:49,200
This is it, Jordan Road.

45
00:03:50,676 --> 00:03:52,747
He was late. . .

46
00:03:52,787 --> 00:03:55,170
He'd been to get a
wedding present, the vase.

47
00:03:56,631 --> 00:03:58,599
Mum always said, "That stupid vase."

48
00:04:02,857 --> 00:04:04,540
He got out of his car.

49
00:04:06,177 --> 00:04:10,591
And crossed the road. . .
Oh, God. This is it.

50
00:04:31,393 --> 00:04:33,441
Go to him. Quick!

51
00:04:42,458 --> 00:04:45,811
It's too late now.

52
00:04:45,851 --> 00:04:49,800
By the time the ambulance
got there, he was dead.

53
00:04:53,646 --> 00:04:55,489
He can't die on his own.

54
00:04:57,043 --> 00:04:58,726
Can I try again?

55
00:05:10,070 --> 00:05:15,784
Right, that's the first you and me.
It's a very bad idea, two sets
of us being here at the same time.

56
00:05:15,824 --> 00:05:22,326
Just be careful they don't see us,
wait till she runs off and he
follows, then go to your dad.

57
00:05:30,646 --> 00:05:32,364
Oh, God. This is it.

58
00:05:38,559 --> 00:05:42,233
I can't do this. You don't have to
do anything you don't want to.

59
00:05:42,273 --> 00:05:44,565
But this is the last time
we can be here.

60
00:05:47,629 --> 00:05:49,756
Rose! No!

61
00:06:02,330 --> 00:06:04,880
I did it.

62
00:06:04,128 --> 00:06:08,644
I saved your life!
Blimey, see the speed of it?
D'you get his number?

63
00:06:08,684 --> 00:06:10,163
I really did it!

64
00:06:10,203 --> 00:06:13,759
Oh, my God, look at you, you're
alive! That car was gonna kill ya!

65
00:06:13,799 --> 00:06:17,556
Give me some credit. I did see it
coming. I wasn't gonna walk under it.

66
00:06:17,596 --> 00:06:19,350
I'm Rose.

67
00:06:21,392 --> 00:06:26,627
Oh, that's a coincidence,
that's my daughter's name.
That's a great name.

68
00:06:26,667 --> 00:06:28,515
Good choice. Well done.

69
00:06:33,058 --> 00:06:34,976
I better shift.
I've got a wedding to go to.

70
00:06:35,016 --> 00:06:38,813
Is that Sarah Clark's wedding?
Yeah. Are you going? Yeah!

71
00:06:38,853 --> 00:06:41,600
Do you and your
boyfriend need a lift?

72
00:06:46,527 --> 00:06:49,234
SCREECHING

73
00:06:51,601 --> 00:06:53,718
Right, there we go.
Sorry about the mess.

74
00:06:53,758 --> 00:06:57,475
If you want a cup of tea, kitchen's
down there, milk's in the fridge,

75
00:06:57,515 --> 00:07:00,878
Well it would be, wouldn't it?
Where else would you put the milk?

76
00:07:00,918 --> 00:07:03,630
Mind you, there's always the
windowsill outside.

77
00:07:03,670 --> 00:07:06,987
If someone invented a windowsill with
special compartments -

78
00:07:07,027 --> 00:07:10,857
one for milk, one for yoghurt -
you could make a lot of money
out of that,

79
00:07:10,897 --> 00:07:16,540
sell it to students and things.
I should write that down.

80
00:07:16,094 --> 00:07:18,682
Anyway, 'scuse me a minute,
gotta go and change.

81
00:07:21,208 --> 00:07:24,365
All the stuff Mum kept. His stuff.

82
00:07:24,405 --> 00:07:28,995
She kept it all packed away in
boxes. She used to show me
when she'd had a bit to drink.

83
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:31,796
Here it is. . .

84
00:07:31,836 --> 00:07:35,448
on display. Where it should be.

85
00:07:39,471 --> 00:07:41,428
Third prize at the bowling.

86
00:07:41,468 --> 00:07:43,328
First two got to go to Didcot.

87
00:07:45,625 --> 00:07:47,377
Health drinks!

88
00:07:47,417 --> 00:07:51,575
Tonics, mum used to call them.
He made his money selling
this Vitex stuff.

89
00:07:51,615 --> 00:07:53,952
He had all sorts
of jobs, he was so clever.

90
00:07:56,609 --> 00:07:58,327
Solar power!

91
00:07:58,367 --> 00:08:01,888
Mum said he was going to do this -
now he can!

92
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:07,205
OK, I'll tell him
you're not my boyfriend.

93
00:08:07,245 --> 00:08:10,597
When we met, I said, "travel
with me in space." You said no.

94
00:08:10,637 --> 00:08:12,274
Then I said, "Time machine".

95
00:08:12,314 --> 00:08:13,910
It wasn't some big plan.

96
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:18,301
I just saw it happening,
and I thought, "I can stop it."

97
00:08:19,825 --> 00:08:24,699
I picked another stupid ape. I should've known. 
It's not about showing you the universe.

98
00:08:24,739 --> 00:08:29,773
It never is. It's about the universe
doing something for you.
So it's OK when YOU go to other times,

99
00:08:29,813 --> 00:08:33,130
and YOU save people's lives,
but not when it's me saving my dad?

100
00:08:33,170 --> 00:08:38,582
I know what I'm doing. You don't! Two sets
of us being there made that a vulnerable point. 
But he's alive.

101
00:08:38,622 --> 00:08:43,335
My entire planet died - my whole family.
Do you think it never occurred to me
to go back and save them?

102
00:08:43,375 --> 00:08:46,130
But it's not like I've 
changed history. Not much.

103
00:08:46,053 --> 00:08:50,930
I mean, he's never gonna be a world leader. 
He's not gonna start 
World War Three or anything.

104
00:08:50,970 --> 00:08:53,766
There's a man alive in the
world who wasn't alive before.

105
00:08:53,806 --> 00:08:57,430
An ordinary man. That's the
most important thing in creation.

106
00:08:57,083 --> 00:09:02,433
The whole world's different because
he's alive. Would you rather him
dead? I'm not saying that. I get it.

107
00:09:02,473 --> 00:09:05,152
For once, YOU'RE not the
most important man in my life!

108
00:09:05,192 --> 00:09:07,987
Let's see how you get on without me.
Give me the key.

109
00:09:08,027 --> 00:09:12,223
The TARDIS key. If I'm so 
insignificant, give it me back!
All right then, I will!

110
00:09:12,263 --> 00:09:15,560
You've got what you wanted,
so that's goodbye then.

111
00:09:16,303 --> 00:09:18,977
You don't scare me!

112
00:09:19,017 --> 00:09:21,494
I know how sad you are!
You'll be back in a minute!

113
00:09:21,534 --> 00:09:24,850
Or will you hang around outside
the TARDIS waiting for me.

114
00:09:24,890 --> 00:09:27,717
And I'll make you wait a long time!

115
00:09:32,925 --> 00:09:35,428
Boyfriend trouble?

116
00:09:43,670 --> 00:09:46,707
WHOOSHING

117
00:09:46,747 --> 00:09:49,779
SCREECHING

118
00:09:56,498 --> 00:09:59,205
MORE SCREECHING

119
00:10:05,330 --> 00:10:07,605
SCREECHING

120
00:10:07,645 --> 00:10:08,843
ARGH!

121
00:10:08,883 --> 00:10:11,443
WHOOSHING

122
00:10:11,483 --> 00:10:13,320
A-ARGH!

123
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:16,392
A-A-ARGH!

124
00:10:20,431 --> 00:10:23,509
'Scuse me, d'you mind?
What are you tidying up for?

125
00:10:23,549 --> 00:10:28,703
Sorry, force of habit.
Don't worry about him.
Couples have rows all the time.

126
00:10:28,743 --> 00:10:31,695
We're not a couple. Why does
everyone think we're a couple?

127
00:10:34,297 --> 00:10:39,294
I think he left me. Pretty girl like
you? If I was going out with you. . .
Stop right there.

128
00:10:39,334 --> 00:10:41,771
I'm just saying. I know what you're
saying

129
00:10:41,811 --> 00:10:45,924
and we're not going there.
At no point are we going anywhere
near there.

130
00:10:45,964 --> 00:10:49,482
You aren't even aware
that "there" exists.

131
00:10:49,522 --> 00:10:55,711
I don't even want to think about
"there", and, believe me, neither
do you. "There", for you, is like,

132
00:10:55,751 --> 00:10:58,300
like the Bermuda Triangle.

133
00:10:58,070 --> 00:11:01,547
Blimey, you know how to
flatter a bloke. Right. Are we off?

134
00:11:01,587 --> 00:11:05,544
So that wouldn't be a
mixed signal at all? Absolutely not.

135
00:11:05,584 --> 00:11:08,300
I'd take you back to the
loony bin where you belong.

136
00:11:08,340 --> 00:11:11,372
I'm sure I've met you somewhere
before.

137
00:11:12,737 --> 00:11:14,989
ORGAN MUSIC

138
00:11:19,488 --> 00:11:22,600
It's weird, there's
so many people missing.

139
00:11:22,046 --> 00:11:25,123
Uncle Steven, Aunty Lynn, all the
Baxters.

140
00:11:25,163 --> 00:11:28,600
I mean, where are they?
You don't think
something's gone wrong?

141
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:31,594
Maybe it's a godsend,
gives you time to think.

142
00:11:31,634 --> 00:11:35,231
You don't have to go through with it.
Live in sin for a bit.

143
00:11:35,271 --> 00:11:38,467
Dad! In ten years' time,
you'll turn round and say,

144
00:11:38,507 --> 00:11:40,844
"If only I could turn the clock
back!"

145
00:11:42,822 --> 00:11:44,710
TINKLING

146
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:48,243
Is it me, or did it just get cold?

147
00:11:49,976 --> 00:11:52,690
BIRDS CAW

148
00:11:54,531 --> 00:11:57,330
LOUD CAWING

149
00:11:57,370 --> 00:12:00,367
WHOOSHING AND SCREECHING

150
00:12:02,725 --> 00:12:04,659
CAWING STOPS

151
00:12:25,618 --> 00:12:27,970
Rose!

152
00:12:28,975 --> 00:12:33,329
I met this bloke at the horses -
he's cutting me in on copyright.
MUSIC ON RADIO

153
00:12:33,369 --> 00:12:36,609
I thought you were
a proper businessman. I wish!

154
00:12:36,649 --> 00:12:39,606
I do a bit of this, bit of that.
I scrape by. Right.

155
00:12:39,646 --> 00:12:41,803
# Never gonna give you up. . . #
I must've heard wrong.

156
00:12:41,843 --> 00:12:44,917
So, really, you're
a bit of a. . .Del Boy.

157
00:12:44,957 --> 00:12:48,808
Oh, shoot me down in flames(!) You're
not related to my wife, are you?

158
00:12:50,271 --> 00:12:52,428
My God. She'll be at the wedding.

159
00:12:52,468 --> 00:12:55,900
What, Jackie? D'you know her?

160
00:12:56,586 --> 00:12:59,262
Sort of.
What's she told you about me, then?

161
00:12:59,302 --> 00:13:04,970
She said she'd picked the most
fantastic man in the world.

162
00:13:04,137 --> 00:13:06,733
Must be a different Jackie.
She'd never say that.

163
00:13:06,773 --> 00:13:09,970
STATIC
# Put your phone back
Quit staring into space. . .#

164
00:13:10,010 --> 00:13:12,480
This stuff goes right over my head.

165
00:13:12,088 --> 00:13:17,203
That's not out yet.
Good job an' all. I'm just gonna
check my messages.

166
00:13:17,243 --> 00:13:19,117
How d'ya mean, messages?

167
00:13:19,157 --> 00:13:22,955
# . .Who decides
whether it's off or on. . . #
Is that a phone? Yeah.

168
00:13:22,995 --> 00:13:26,310
# . .Now the girl's rude
I know she's rude. . . #

169
00:13:26,071 --> 00:13:28,349
'Watson, come here. I need you.'

170
00:13:28,389 --> 00:13:30,867
'Watson, come here. I need you.

171
00:13:30,907 --> 00:13:35,133
'Watson, come here. I need you.'

172
00:13:48,332 --> 00:13:50,369
Half the guests haven't turned up!

173
00:13:50,409 --> 00:13:54,923
You're better off not being here.
It's a disaster in the making.

174
00:13:54,963 --> 00:13:58,197
No, in this case "knocked her up"
IS a phrase I'd use!

175
00:13:58,237 --> 00:14:00,994
'Watson, come here. I need you.'

176
00:14:01,034 --> 00:14:04,660
Hello? Who's this?
'Watson, come here. I n. . .'

177
00:14:06,309 --> 00:14:10,225
Dad, get inside!
We can't see the bride before
the wedding. It's bad luck!

178
00:14:10,265 --> 00:14:14,821
Bad luck when you met her!
I tell you, this day is cursed!

179
00:14:24,250 --> 00:14:27,170
Now that's what I call a meringue!

180
00:14:27,210 --> 00:14:31,602
Stuart's dad said, go round
the block cos there's people
missing. Missing?!

181
00:14:31,642 --> 00:14:34,399
There's no Dave, no Sunita, no Bea.

182
00:14:34,439 --> 00:14:39,236
No-one from the Lamb and Flag.
Oh, my train's detached again.
I knew I should have used Velcro!

183
00:14:39,276 --> 00:14:42,629
I'm here. Stop your bellyaching!
Take Rose a sec, will you?

184
00:14:42,669 --> 00:14:45,666
Oh, ain't she pretty?
She's a little madam!

185
00:14:45,706 --> 00:14:50,376
Oh, I need more hands! Where's her
useless article of a dad got to?

186
00:14:58,575 --> 00:15:01,452
Dad!
TYRES SCREECH

187
00:15:01,492 --> 00:15:05,388
It was that car. Same one as before.

188
00:15:08,204 --> 00:15:09,967
Where's he gone?

189
00:15:12,077 --> 00:15:15,277
You called me dad? !
What did you say that for?

190
00:15:15,317 --> 00:15:18,630
Oh, wonderful! Here he is,
the accident waiting to happen!

191
00:15:18,670 --> 00:15:21,588
You'd be late for your own
funeral and it nearly was!

192
00:15:21,628 --> 00:15:23,840
No damage done. And who's this?

193
00:15:24,948 --> 00:15:27,182
What you looking at
with your mouth open? !

194
00:15:27,222 --> 00:15:31,577
Your hair.
What? I've never seen it like. . .

195
00:15:31,617 --> 00:15:33,896
I mean. . .

196
00:15:33,936 --> 00:15:36,729
It's lovely. Your hair's lovely.

197
00:15:40,490 --> 00:15:43,527
And that baby you're holding. . .

198
00:15:43,567 --> 00:15:45,524
That would be. . .

199
00:15:45,564 --> 00:15:47,241
your baby.

200
00:15:47,281 --> 00:15:49,877
Another one of yours, is she?
She saved my life!

201
00:15:49,917 --> 00:15:52,595
Ah, that's a new one.
What was it last time?

202
00:15:52,635 --> 00:15:54,832
I didn't even know her!
She was a cloakroom attendant!

203
00:15:54,872 --> 00:15:56,630
I was helping her look for my ticket.

204
00:15:56,670 --> 00:16:00,229
There were 3 duffle coats all the
same. Somehow the rack collapsed.

205
00:16:00,269 --> 00:16:03,346
We were under all this stuff.
Were you playing around? !

206
00:16:03,386 --> 00:16:05,504
What's it got to do with you?

207
00:16:05,544 --> 00:16:08,657
What does he get up to? You'd know!
Oh, cos I'm that stupid.

208
00:16:08,697 --> 00:16:11,298
I play around, and bring her to
meet the missus!

209
00:16:11,338 --> 00:16:12,854
But you ARE that stupid!

210
00:16:12,894 --> 00:16:15,132
Can we keep this stuff for back home?

211
00:16:15,172 --> 00:16:18,807
What, with the rest of the rubbish?
You bring home cut-price detergents,

212
00:16:18,847 --> 00:16:24,798
tonic water,
Betamax tapes, and none of it works.
I'm drowning in your rubbish.

213
00:16:24,838 --> 00:16:28,117
What did he tell you? Did he
say he's this big businessman?

214
00:16:28,157 --> 00:16:32,234
Cos he's not, he's a failure. Born
failure, that one! Rose needs a
proper father,

215
00:16:32,274 --> 00:16:37,843
not one who's playing about like a
big kid. . . I'm making a living. It
keeps us fed, doesn't it? Stop it!

216
00:16:39,664 --> 00:16:42,262
You're not like this.
You love each other.

217
00:16:42,302 --> 00:16:46,218
Oh, Pete!
You never used to like them mental.
Or I don't know, maybe you did.

218
00:16:46,258 --> 00:16:51,216
Jackie, listen. . . If you're
not careful, there'll be a wedding
and a divorce on the same day.

219
00:16:51,256 --> 00:16:56,248
Wait here. Give us a minute with the
missus. Straighten the car up, or
stick it round the corner!

220
00:16:56,288 --> 00:16:59,320
Don't cause any more trouble!

221
00:17:16,106 --> 00:17:19,183
WHOOSHING

222
00:17:19,223 --> 00:17:22,300
Hey Jack. Jack. . .

223
00:17:30,254 --> 00:17:32,142
WHOOSHING

224
00:17:35,925 --> 00:17:38,223
WHOOSHING

225
00:17:49,634 --> 00:17:52,788
I'm not listening, it's the duffle
coats all over again.

226
00:17:52,828 --> 00:17:56,240
Jackie, sometimes a duffle
coat is just a duffle coat.

227
00:17:56,064 --> 00:18:00,419
Things will get better soon.
I promise. I've had enough of all
your daft schemes.

228
00:18:00,459 --> 00:18:06,333
I never know where the next meal's
coming from. I'll get it right, love
I promise you, one day soon.

229
00:18:06,373 --> 00:18:08,585
Come on!

230
00:18:10,930 --> 00:18:13,166
Monsters! Going to eat us!

231
00:18:13,206 --> 00:18:16,932
What sort of monsters, sweetheart?
Is it aliens?

232
00:18:19,957 --> 00:18:22,175
Rose!

233
00:18:26,952 --> 00:18:29,659
Get in the church!

234
00:18:33,303 --> 00:18:35,743
ROARING

235
00:18:35,783 --> 00:18:37,392
GASPING

236
00:18:39,697 --> 00:18:41,346
Get in the church!

237
00:18:45,851 --> 00:18:49,848
Oh, my God! What are they? !
What are they? ! Inside!

238
00:18:49,888 --> 00:18:51,528
SCREECHING

239
00:18:51,568 --> 00:18:54,522
Sarah! Stay in there!

240
00:18:54,562 --> 00:18:56,615
SCREECHING

241
00:18:59,834 --> 00:19:03,950
THEY SCREECH
Aargh! Aargh!

242
00:19:03,990 --> 00:19:06,578
SHE SCREAMS

243
00:19:08,268 --> 00:19:09,824
A-argh!

244
00:19:09,864 --> 00:19:11,464
In!

245
00:19:11,504 --> 00:19:15,990
BABY CRIES
SCREECHING AND ROARING

246
00:19:15,139 --> 00:19:17,980
WHOOSHING

247
00:19:17,138 --> 00:19:18,854
SCREECHING

248
00:19:18,894 --> 00:19:23,120
SCREECHING CONTINUES

249
00:19:27,847 --> 00:19:30,601
They can't get in.
Old windows and doors. OK?

250
00:19:30,641 --> 00:19:33,321
The older something
is, the stronger it is.

251
00:19:33,361 --> 00:19:35,518
What else?

252
00:19:35,558 --> 00:19:37,874
SCREECHING

253
00:19:37,914 --> 00:19:41,472
Go and check the other doors. Move!
What's happening? !

254
00:19:41,512 --> 00:19:44,826
What are they? ! There's been an
accident in time. A wound in time.

255
00:19:44,866 --> 00:19:47,660
They're like bacteria, taking
advantage.

256
00:19:47,106 --> 00:19:49,262
What d'you mean, "time"?

257
00:19:49,302 --> 00:19:51,600
I might've known you'd argue.

258
00:19:51,100 --> 00:19:54,736
Jackie, I'm sick of you
complaining. . . How d'you know my
name?

259
00:19:54,776 --> 00:19:59,540
No, and you never will,
unless I sort this out. I've waited
a long time to say this.

260
00:19:59,094 --> 00:20:03,928
Jackie Tyler. Do as I say.
Go and check the doors!

261
00:20:03,968 --> 00:20:07,482
Yes, sir.
I should've done that ages ago.
My dad was out there.

262
00:20:07,522 --> 00:20:11,720
We've got to concentrate on keeping
ourselves alive. My dad had. . .

263
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:15,793
There is nothing I can do for him.
No, but he had this phone thing.

264
00:20:15,833 --> 00:20:22,270
I can't get it to work. I keep
getting this voice. 'Watson, come
here. I need you. Watson. . .'

265
00:20:22,067 --> 00:20:25,424
That's the very first
phone call. Alexander Graham Bell.

266
00:20:25,464 --> 00:20:29,620
The telephone won't be much use. But
someone must've called the police!

267
00:20:29,102 --> 00:20:34,150
The police can't help
you now. No-one can. Nothing in this
universe can harm those things.

268
00:20:34,055 --> 00:20:38,440
Time's been damaged, and they've
come to sterilise the wound. . .

269
00:20:41,926 --> 00:20:45,164
 .by consuming everything inside.

270
00:20:45,204 --> 00:20:47,257
Is this because. . .

271
00:20:48,837 --> 00:20:50,646
Is this my fault?

272
00:20:58,029 --> 00:21:01,146
SCREECHING

273
00:21:04,543 --> 00:21:06,841
NO!

274
00:21:21,202 --> 00:21:24,162
There's smoke coming up
from the city, but no sirens.

275
00:21:24,202 --> 00:21:27,835
I don't think it's just us. I think
these things are all over the place.

276
00:21:27,875 --> 00:21:29,518
Maybe the whole world.

277
00:21:37,308 --> 00:21:40,504
Was that a car?

278
00:21:40,544 --> 00:21:42,501
It's not important.

279
00:21:42,541 --> 00:21:44,469
Don't worry about it.

280
00:21:50,773 --> 00:21:52,866
SCREECHING

281
00:22:08,833 --> 00:22:11,710
This mate of yours. . .

282
00:22:11,111 --> 00:22:12,914
What did he mean, this is your fault?

283
00:22:14,428 --> 00:22:17,703
Dunno. Just. . .everything.

284
00:22:17,743 --> 00:22:20,100
I gave you my car keys.

285
00:22:23,219 --> 00:22:26,211
You don't give your keys
to a complete stranger.

286
00:22:28,493 --> 00:22:31,691
It's like I trusted you.

287
00:22:31,731 --> 00:22:33,611
Moment I met you.

288
00:22:33,651 --> 00:22:36,230
I just did.

289
00:22:37,165 --> 00:22:39,542
A wound in time.

290
00:22:41,519 --> 00:22:43,314
You called me Dad.

291
00:22:43,354 --> 00:22:45,453
I can see it.

292
00:22:46,953 --> 00:22:49,149
In my eyes.

293
00:22:49,189 --> 00:22:51,270
Jackie's attitude.

294
00:22:51,067 --> 00:22:52,915
You sound like her when you shout.

295
00:23:02,419 --> 00:23:05,655
You are! You are. You're my Rose.

296
00:23:05,695 --> 00:23:08,451
You're my Rose grown up!

297
00:23:08,491 --> 00:23:10,689
Dad.

298
00:23:10,729 --> 00:23:13,300
My dad. . .

299
00:23:13,043 --> 00:23:14,652
My daddy. . .

300
00:23:17,637 --> 00:23:23,872
Excuse me. Mr. . .um? Doctor.
You seem to know what's going on.
I give that impression, yeah.

301
00:23:23,912 --> 00:23:27,239
I just wanted to ask. . .
Can you save us?

302
00:23:33,504 --> 00:23:37,980
Who are you two, then?
Stuart Hoskins. Sarah Clark.

303
00:23:37,138 --> 00:23:39,697
And one extra, boy or girl?

304
00:23:39,737 --> 00:23:43,414
I dunno. I don't want to know,
really.

305
00:23:43,454 --> 00:23:46,110
How did all this get started?

306
00:23:46,051 --> 00:23:48,404
Outside the Beatbox Club, 2am.

307
00:23:48,444 --> 00:23:52,420
Street corner, I'd lost my purse,
didn't have money for a taxi.

308
00:23:52,082 --> 00:23:54,239
I took her home.

309
00:23:54,279 --> 00:23:56,959
Then what? Asked her for a date?

310
00:23:56,999 --> 00:23:59,396
Wrote his number on
the back of my hand.

311
00:23:59,436 --> 00:24:02,877
Never got rid of her since!
My dad said. . .

312
00:24:06,470 --> 00:24:11,423
I don't know what this is all about,
and I know we're not important. . .

313
00:24:11,463 --> 00:24:13,266
Who said you're not important?

314
00:24:14,978 --> 00:24:19,533
I've travelled to all sorts
of places, done things
you couldn't even imagine, but. . .

315
00:24:19,573 --> 00:24:20,807
you two. . .

316
00:24:22,292 --> 00:24:25,490
Street corner, two in the morning,

317
00:24:25,089 --> 00:24:27,970
getting a taxi home. . .

318
00:24:28,643 --> 00:24:30,680
I've never had a life like that.

319
00:24:30,720 --> 00:24:32,440
Yes!

320
00:24:32,480 --> 00:24:35,227
I'll try and save you.

321
00:24:39,071 --> 00:24:41,511
I'm a dad.

322
00:24:41,551 --> 00:24:43,387
I mean, I'm already a dad, but. . .

323
00:24:43,427 --> 00:24:45,426
Rose grows up and. . .

324
00:24:45,466 --> 00:24:46,866
she's you!

325
00:24:46,906 --> 00:24:48,506
That's wonderful.

326
00:24:48,546 --> 00:24:53,455
I suppose I thought. . .
that you'd be a bit useless,
with my useless genes.

327
00:24:55,296 --> 00:24:57,940
But how did you get here?

328
00:24:57,134 --> 00:24:59,494
Do you really wanna know? Yeah.

329
00:24:59,534 --> 00:25:01,906
A time machine.

330
00:25:02,969 --> 00:25:04,249
Time machine?

331
00:25:04,289 --> 00:25:05,569
Cross my heart.

332
00:25:05,609 --> 00:25:09,959
Do you all have time machines where
you come from? No, just the Doctor.

333
00:25:09,999 --> 00:25:14,756
Did you know these. . .
these things were coming? No.

334
00:25:14,796 --> 00:25:17,800
God, I dunno, my head's spinning.

335
00:25:19,949 --> 00:25:21,632
What's the future like?

336
00:25:23,507 --> 00:25:25,302
It's not so different.

337
00:25:25,342 --> 00:25:28,890
What am I like? Have I gone grey?

338
00:25:31,858 --> 00:25:33,538
Have I gone bald?

339
00:25:33,578 --> 00:25:35,381
Don't tell me I've gone bald!

340
00:25:40,685 --> 00:25:44,880
So, if this mate of yours isn't your
boyfriend, I have to say I'm glad,

341
00:25:44,920 --> 00:25:47,920
cos being your dad,
I think he's a bit old for you!

342
00:25:47,960 --> 00:25:51,572
Have you got a bloke?
No. I did have. Mickey!

343
00:25:55,590 --> 00:25:59,748
Do you know him? I just. . .
didn't recognise him in a suit.

344
00:25:59,788 --> 00:26:02,985
You have to let go of me,
sweetheart.
 .I'm always saying that.

345
00:26:03,025 --> 00:26:08,298
He grabs hold of what's passing and
holds on for dear life. God help
his poor girlfriend, if he gets one.

346
00:26:08,338 --> 00:26:10,560
Me and Rose were just talking.

347
00:26:10,096 --> 00:26:16,290
Oh, yeah, talking? While the world
comes to an end, what do you do?
Cling to the youngest blonde!

348
00:26:17,529 --> 00:26:19,736
Come on, Mick!

349
00:26:21,881 --> 00:26:24,559
You can't tell her. Why?

350
00:26:24,599 --> 00:26:27,875
I don't want you to tell her.
You don't want people to know?

351
00:26:27,915 --> 00:26:33,313
Where I come from, Jackie doesn't
know how to work the video recorder.
I showed her that last week!

352
00:26:33,353 --> 00:26:35,389
Point taken.

353
00:26:35,429 --> 00:26:37,607
SCREECHING

354
00:26:39,984 --> 00:26:45,181
Now, Rose, you're not going to bring
about the end of the world, are you?

355
00:26:45,221 --> 00:26:46,250
Are you?

356
00:26:51,855 --> 00:26:54,486
Jackie gave her to me to look after.

357
00:26:54,526 --> 00:26:56,566
How times change.

358
00:26:56,606 --> 00:27:02,800
I'd better be careful.
I think I just imprinted myself on
Mickey like a mother chicken.

359
00:27:02,120 --> 00:27:05,436
No! Don't touch the baby.
SCREECHING CONTINUES

360
00:27:08,591 --> 00:27:13,747
You're the same person, that's
a paradox. We don't want that,
not with these things outside.

361
00:27:13,787 --> 00:27:17,743
Anything new, any disturbance
in time, makes them stronger.

362
00:27:17,783 --> 00:27:20,102
A paradox might let them in.

363
00:27:20,142 --> 00:27:22,219
I can't do anything right, can I?

364
00:27:22,259 --> 00:27:24,576
Since you asked, no. So. . .

365
00:27:24,616 --> 00:27:27,416
don't touch the baby!

366
00:27:27,456 --> 00:27:30,533
I'm not stupid! You could've
fooled me.

367
00:27:34,003 --> 00:27:36,602
All right,

368
00:27:36,642 --> 00:27:38,331
I'm sorry.

369
00:27:40,358 --> 00:27:43,836
I wasn't really going to
leave you on your own. I know.

370
00:27:43,876 --> 00:27:46,512
Between you and me,
I haven't got a plan.

371
00:27:46,552 --> 00:27:48,430
No idea.

372
00:27:48,470 --> 00:27:51,230
No way out.

373
00:27:51,270 --> 00:27:53,587
You'll think of something.

374
00:27:53,627 --> 00:27:55,303
The entire Earth's been sterilised.

375
00:27:55,343 --> 00:27:59,301
This, and other places like it, are
all that's left of the human race.

376
00:27:59,341 --> 00:28:04,550
We might hold out for a while,
but nothing can stop them.
They'll get through,

377
00:28:04,095 --> 00:28:06,252
the walls aren't that old.

378
00:28:06,292 --> 00:28:09,900
And there's nothing I can do
to stop them.

379
00:28:09,049 --> 00:28:14,762
There used to be laws stopping
this kind of thing from happening.
My people would have stopped this.

380
00:28:14,802 --> 00:28:17,480
But they're all gone.

381
00:28:17,520 --> 00:28:19,876
And now I'm going the same way.

382
00:28:19,916 --> 00:28:21,434
If I'd realised. . .

383
00:28:24,354 --> 00:28:25,833
Just. . .

384
00:28:26,991 --> 00:28:29,733
 .tell me you're sorry.

385
00:28:30,827 --> 00:28:32,347
I am.

386
00:28:32,387 --> 00:28:34,963
I'm sorry.

387
00:28:45,654 --> 00:28:46,967
Doc?

388
00:28:47,007 --> 00:28:49,600
Have you got something hot?

389
00:28:50,163 --> 00:28:51,312
Agh!

390
00:28:52,523 --> 00:28:54,161
It's the TARDIS key!

391
00:29:01,711 --> 00:29:04,949
It's telling me it's still
connected to the TARDIS!

392
00:29:04,989 --> 00:29:09,464
The inside of my ship was thrown out
of the wound, but we can use this
to bring it back.

393
00:29:09,504 --> 00:29:15,898
And once I've got my ship back,
I can mend everything! I just need
power. Has anybody got a battery?

394
00:29:15,938 --> 00:29:17,422
This one big enough?

395
00:29:19,213 --> 00:29:22,249
Fantastic! Good old dad.
There you go.

396
00:29:22,289 --> 00:29:25,966
Just need to do a bit
of charging up,

397
00:29:26,006 --> 00:29:29,367
and then we can bring everyone back.

398
00:29:31,519 --> 00:29:34,306
SCREECHING, CRASH AT DOOR

399
00:29:35,914 --> 00:29:38,701
SCREECHING AND CRASHING CONTINUES

400
00:29:51,619 --> 00:29:56,960
You, um, you never said why you
came here in the first place.

401
00:29:56,136 --> 00:30:01,887
If I had a time machine, I wouldn't
have thought 1987 was anything
special. Not round 'ere, anyway!

402
00:30:01,927 --> 00:30:03,487
We just ended up 'ere.

403
00:30:03,527 --> 00:30:06,999
Lucky for me, eh?
If you hadn't been there to save me.

404
00:30:07,039 --> 00:30:11,838
That was just a coincidence.
That was just really good luck.

405
00:30:11,878 --> 00:30:13,362
It's amazing.

406
00:30:16,032 --> 00:30:20,332
So, in the future, um, are me
and her indoors still together?

407
00:30:21,349 --> 00:30:23,260
Yeah.

408
00:30:23,066 --> 00:30:25,302
You still livin' with us?

409
00:30:25,342 --> 00:30:26,906
Yep.

410
00:30:30,461 --> 00:30:32,190
Am I a good dad?

411
00:30:35,769 --> 00:30:37,293
You. . .

412
00:30:39,726 --> 00:30:43,683
You told me a bedtime story
every night, when I was small.

413
00:30:43,723 --> 00:30:47,540
You were always there,
you never missed one.

414
00:30:51,714 --> 00:30:53,363
And. . .um. . .

415
00:30:55,229 --> 00:30:59,620
 .you took us for picnics
in the country every Saturday.

416
00:31:00,548 --> 00:31:02,470
You never let us down.

417
00:31:04,221 --> 00:31:07,590
You were there for us all the time.

418
00:31:07,099 --> 00:31:09,926
Someone I could really rely on.

419
00:31:16,370 --> 00:31:17,883
That's not me.

420
00:31:38,305 --> 00:31:42,701
Right, no-one touches that key!
Have you got that? Don't touch it.

421
00:31:42,741 --> 00:31:46,217
Anyone touches that key,
it'll be. . .well, zap!

422
00:31:46,257 --> 00:31:51,211
Leave it be, and everything
will be fine. We'll get out of here.
All of us. Stuart. Sarah!

423
00:31:51,251 --> 00:31:53,529
You're gonna get married,
just like I said!

424
00:31:53,569 --> 00:31:55,827
SCREECHING

425
00:32:02,560 --> 00:32:05,222
THRUMMING

426
00:32:19,900 --> 00:32:23,338
When time gets sorted. . . Everybody
here forgets what happened.

427
00:32:23,378 --> 00:32:27,650
And don't worry, the thing that
you changed will. . .stay changed.

428
00:32:27,690 --> 00:32:30,329
You mean, I'll still be alive.

429
00:32:30,369 --> 00:32:32,866
Though I'm meant to be dead.

430
00:32:37,439 --> 00:32:41,438
That's why I've haven't done anything
with my life. Why it meant nothing.

431
00:32:41,478 --> 00:32:43,874
Doesn't work like that. Rubbish.

432
00:32:43,914 --> 00:32:47,231
I was so useless,
I couldn't even die properly!

433
00:32:47,271 --> 00:32:50,827
Now it's my fault all this
has happened. This is my fault.

434
00:32:50,867 --> 00:32:52,467
No, love.

435
00:32:52,507 --> 00:32:54,103
I'm your dad.

436
00:32:54,143 --> 00:32:56,303
It's my job for it to be my fault.

437
00:32:56,343 --> 00:32:57,622
Her dad?

438
00:32:58,543 --> 00:33:00,340
How are you her dad?

439
00:33:01,537 --> 00:33:03,134
How old were you, 12?

440
00:33:03,174 --> 00:33:04,812
Oh, that's disgusting!

441
00:33:04,852 --> 00:33:07,487
Jacks, listen.

442
00:33:07,527 --> 00:33:09,445
This is Rose.

443
00:33:09,485 --> 00:33:15,559
Rose? ! How sick is that? ! Did you
give my daughter a second-hand name?
How many are there?

444
00:33:15,599 --> 00:33:19,575
Do you call them all Rose? ! Oh, for
God's sake, look! It's the same Rose.

445
00:33:21,113 --> 00:33:22,922
Rose! No!

446
00:33:25,429 --> 00:33:28,910
THEY ALL SCREAM
Everyone, behind me!

447
00:33:41,770 --> 00:33:44,523
I'm the oldest thing in here!

448
00:33:46,686 --> 00:33:48,938
Doctor!

449
00:34:11,343 --> 00:34:13,610
Cold. . .

450
00:34:14,738 --> 00:34:16,387
He's cold.

451
00:34:20,771 --> 00:34:23,230
Oh, my God, he's dead.

452
00:34:27,482 --> 00:34:29,682
It's all my fault.

453
00:34:29,722 --> 00:34:32,598
Both of you! All of you!

454
00:34:32,638 --> 00:34:35,760
The whole world! Ah, come on!

455
00:34:35,116 --> 00:34:37,863
SCREECHING RESUMES

456
00:34:44,147 --> 00:34:48,368
This is it. There's nothing
we can do. It's the end.

457
00:35:55,069 --> 00:35:58,270
The Doctor really cared about you.

458
00:35:58,067 --> 00:36:03,105
He didn't want you to go through it
again, not if there was another way.

459
00:36:03,145 --> 00:36:05,198
Now there isn't.

460
00:36:07,817 --> 00:36:09,972
What are you talking about?

461
00:36:10,012 --> 00:36:14,648
The car that should have killed me,
love. It's here.

462
00:36:18,524 --> 00:36:21,920
The Doctor worked it out way back,
but he, um. . .

463
00:36:21,960 --> 00:36:24,753
he tried to protect me.

464
00:36:27,555 --> 00:36:29,394
Still. . .

465
00:36:29,434 --> 00:36:31,910
He's not in charge any more.

466
00:36:31,950 --> 00:36:33,190
I am.

467
00:36:33,230 --> 00:36:35,948
But you can't. . .

468
00:36:35,988 --> 00:36:38,690
Who am I, love?

469
00:36:39,703 --> 00:36:41,227
My daddy.

470
00:36:44,178 --> 00:36:46,385
Jackie, look at her.

471
00:36:47,931 --> 00:36:49,785
She's ours.

472
00:36:56,685 --> 00:36:58,403
Oh, God.

473
00:37:05,076 --> 00:37:07,453
I'm meant to be dead, Jackie.

474
00:37:08,592 --> 00:37:10,669
You're gonna get rid of me at last.

475
00:37:10,709 --> 00:37:12,228
Don't say that.

476
00:37:12,268 --> 00:37:15,508
For once in your life, trust me.
It's gotta be done.

477
00:37:15,548 --> 00:37:19,433
You've got to survive, cos you've
got to bring up our daughter.

478
00:37:33,003 --> 00:37:37,638
I never read those bedtime stories,
I never took you on those picnics.

479
00:37:37,678 --> 00:37:39,398
I was never there for you.

480
00:37:39,438 --> 00:37:41,917
You would've been.

481
00:37:41,957 --> 00:37:44,794
But I can do this for you.

482
00:37:44,834 --> 00:37:46,711
I can be a proper dad to you now.

483
00:37:46,751 --> 00:37:48,351
It's not fair.

484
00:37:48,391 --> 00:37:50,948
I've had all these extra hours.

485
00:37:50,988 --> 00:37:53,946
No-one else in the world
has ever had that.

486
00:37:53,986 --> 00:37:55,862
And on top of that,

487
00:37:55,902 --> 00:37:57,420
I get to see you.

488
00:38:00,179 --> 00:38:02,761
And you're beautiful.

489
00:38:05,170 --> 00:38:07,646
How lucky am I, eh?

490
00:38:07,686 --> 00:38:10,843
So, come on, do as your dad says.

491
00:38:19,405 --> 00:38:21,282
You gonna be there for me, love?

492
00:38:25,188 --> 00:38:26,917
Thanks for saving me.

493
00:38:32,863 --> 00:38:35,286
SCREECHING

494
00:38:57,154 --> 00:38:59,274
TYRES SCREECH

495
00:38:59,314 --> 00:39:01,526
Goodbye, love.

496
00:39:18,139 --> 00:39:20,391
Go to him. Quick.

497
00:40:10,517 --> 00:40:13,402
The driver was just a kid.

498
00:40:13,442 --> 00:40:15,511
He stopped,

499
00:40:15,551 --> 00:40:17,311
he waited for the police.

500
00:40:17,351 --> 00:40:19,267
It wasn't his fault.

501
00:40:19,307 --> 00:40:22,134
For some reason, Pete just ran out.

502
00:40:24,710 --> 00:40:26,899
People say there was this girl. . .

503
00:40:26,939 --> 00:40:32,348
And she sat with Pete while
he was dying. She held his hand.

504
00:40:33,930 --> 00:40:35,989
Then she was gone.

505
00:40:37,217 --> 00:40:40,687
Never found out who she was.

506
00:41:05,978 --> 00:41:08,254
'Peter Alan Tyler, my dad.

507
00:41:08,294 --> 00:41:11,870
'The most wonderful man
in the world.

508
00:41:12,491 --> 00:41:15,198
'Died the 7th of November 1987.'

509
00:41:31,274 --> 00:41:32,629
And why are we chasing it?

510
00:41:32,669 --> 00:41:37,134
It's mauve and dangerous, and about
30 seconds from the centre of London.

511
00:41:39,702 --> 00:41:41,841
Rose! Please let me in, Mummy.

512
00:41:42,903 --> 00:41:43,847
You mustn't let him touch ya!
Are you a doctor? I have my moments.

513
00:41:43,863 --> 00:41:46,115
You mustn't let him touch ya!
Are you a doctor? I have my moments.

514
00:41:46,573 --> 00:41:50,330
They've all got the same injuries.
Right down to the scar on the back of their hand.

515
00:41:50,370 --> 00:41:54,291
Physical injuries. . .as plague.

516
00:41:54,331 --> 00:41:58,601
Hello. Hello.
Shall we have a drink on the balcony?

517
00:41:58,961 --> 00:42:00,201
Ooh!

518
00:42:00,241 --> 00:42:04,581
Ooh, I like to think of myself
as a criminal. I bet you do!

519
00:42:05,720 --> 00:42:07,654
SHE SCREAMS

